1
00:00:01,710 --> 00:00:03,712
[♪ western music plays]

2
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:07,341
[narrator, in English] A few months ago.

3
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:16,808
Monument Valley, Utah.

4
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:38,830
[Noel, in Japanese] Chaka, Umi, Shizu.

5
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:43,710
I came up with this itinerary
to share this majestic scenery with you.

6
00:00:46,838 --> 00:00:48,298
[narrator] It's the special summer break

7
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:50,175
Noel organized
for the Travis Japan members.

8
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,972
At the foot of the Rocky Mountains
where the desert winds blow

9
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:59,351
lies the real America
the members have yet to see.

10
00:01:03,188 --> 00:01:06,316
The Travis Japan members except for Noel
divide up into two groups

11
00:01:07,109 --> 00:01:08,944
and head to their final destination.
Sedona, Arizona.

12
00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:14,700
Noel sets out to Sedona on his own
before the others

13
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,744
to quietly prepare
a warm welcome for them.

14
00:01:19,037 --> 00:01:21,164
[♪ opening theme music plays]

15
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:37,389
Travis Japan
SUMMER VACATION!! IN THE USA

16
00:01:42,352 --> 00:01:45,314
[in Japanese] The vast and breathtaking
Monument Valley is on a Navajo tribe land.

17
00:01:45,397 --> 00:01:49,568
It is located on the
southern Utah-northern Arizona border.

18
00:01:51,069 --> 00:01:53,280
This classic American landscape is defined

19
00:01:53,363 --> 00:01:55,866
by the towering sandstones
known as "buttes,"

20
00:01:55,949 --> 00:01:58,452
which can reach
up to 300 meters in height.

21
00:01:58,952 --> 00:02:01,455
This place is famous for its role
in various films.

22
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,041
All right.

23
00:02:06,168 --> 00:02:07,419
This is the Utah landscape.

24
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:10,922
Hurry up.

25
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:13,884
[Umi] What's going on, Kaito?

26
00:02:15,344 --> 00:02:16,345
What are you doing?

27
00:02:19,765 --> 00:02:21,350
This is where Forrest Gump was filmed.

28
00:02:23,852 --> 00:02:24,853
The breeze!

29
00:02:26,396 --> 00:02:29,191
This is the very road Forrest runs on.

30
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:32,569
That's right.
Forrest Gump zipped right through here.

31
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:33,987
[in English] "My name is Forrest Gump."

32
00:02:35,072 --> 00:02:37,908
Your name is Chaka.

33
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:39,576
You are not Forrest Gump.

34
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,203
Hi, my name is Forrest Gump.

35
00:02:41,286 --> 00:02:42,537
-[Umi, in Japanese] No, no.
-[Shizu laughs]

36
00:02:42,621 --> 00:02:45,040
-Care if I recreate the iconic scene?
-What scene?

37
00:02:46,333 --> 00:02:47,334
What are you about to do?

38
00:02:48,794 --> 00:02:49,795
Ready?

39
00:02:50,837 --> 00:02:52,839
[Umi] Yeah. Go for it.

40
00:02:55,342 --> 00:02:56,343
Kaito?

41
00:02:57,094 --> 00:02:58,095
See ya!

42
00:02:59,096 --> 00:03:00,681
-[Umi] Wanna chase him?
-[Shizu] Should we?

43
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:08,355
[♪ upbeat music plays]

44
00:03:16,905 --> 00:03:20,075
I have America in the palm of my hands.

45
00:03:20,158 --> 00:03:21,159
[laughs]

46
00:03:22,369 --> 00:03:24,371
[♪ upbeat music continues]

47
00:03:57,988 --> 00:03:58,989
[♪ music ends]

48
00:03:59,573 --> 00:04:02,367
[Umi] There's way more visitors here
than the other campsite.

49
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:04,327
-[Shizu] You mean, compared to Moab?
-[Umi] Yeah.

50
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:06,872
-[Chaka] We're going to camp out tonight.
-Okay.

51
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:07,956
[Umi] Finally?

52
00:04:08,039 --> 00:04:10,709
We're finally going to do it.
We're sleeping in a tent, not the RV.

53
00:04:10,792 --> 00:04:11,960
-A tent?
-Here it comes!

54
00:04:12,544 --> 00:04:14,296
[Shizu] Love the camp vibe.

55
00:04:14,379 --> 00:04:15,964
[Chaka] This is really starting
to feel like camping.

56
00:04:16,047 --> 00:04:17,883
[Umi] This is the most authentic
camp setup we've had so far.

57
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:19,050
[Chaka] Gimme a sec.

58
00:04:19,634 --> 00:04:22,095
We have leftover sausage links
from the grocery run.

59
00:04:22,179 --> 00:04:23,930
-Good idea. Let's toss it in.
-We're using it?

60
00:04:24,014 --> 00:04:25,348
It'll turn out delicious.

61
00:04:32,564 --> 00:04:34,649
Then this one… I'll do it once Umi's done.

62
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:35,734
Got it.

63
00:04:37,152 --> 00:04:38,737
We might have a cutting board.

64
00:04:40,071 --> 00:04:41,072
[Chaka] Nice.

65
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:43,575
[Umi] Okay!

66
00:04:44,201 --> 00:04:46,870
Can we make vegetable stir fry
with sausage?

67
00:04:47,662 --> 00:04:48,663
[Shizu] Is that the sausage?

68
00:04:52,834 --> 00:04:54,419
[Umi] Gotta use up the leftovers.

69
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:04,638
I never know how many layers
I'm supposed to peel.

70
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:06,932
[Shizu] To the white layer is fine.

71
00:05:07,015 --> 00:05:09,142
-[Umi] White is good? Not green?
-[Shizu] White or the green. That's right.

72
00:05:09,226 --> 00:05:10,227
-How about this?
-You got it.

73
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,979
[Umi] Hold on, how can you see its color
through those shades?

74
00:05:13,063 --> 00:05:14,147
-I can see it, I promise.
-[Umi] Hey!

75
00:05:14,231 --> 00:05:16,149
-My eyes are sensitive from the sun…
-You've peeled it enough.

76
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:17,317
…so I have to keep them on.

77
00:05:19,069 --> 00:05:20,570
[Umi] Let's see…

78
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:22,823
This means I'm in charge
of the cutlery, yeah?

79
00:05:25,826 --> 00:05:27,410
[Chaka] Let's load it up. There.

80
00:05:36,169 --> 00:05:37,546
[Shizu] Ta-da!

81
00:05:37,629 --> 00:05:40,507
-[Chaka] Bon appétit.
-[Umi] Sautéed sausage, peppers and onion.

82
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:41,842
[Chaka] Yum-yum, it's da best.

83
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:45,053
Thanks for the impeccable teamwork, guys.

84
00:05:45,762 --> 00:05:46,930
Hands together.

85
00:05:47,013 --> 00:05:48,723
-Thanks for the meal.
-Thanks for the meal.

86
00:05:48,807 --> 00:05:49,933
Well then…

87
00:05:50,016 --> 00:05:51,726
-[in English] Cheers!
-Cheers!

88
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:57,899
-[in Japanese] Well…
-I'm craving curry.

89
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,818
-Time to dig in.
-Time to eat.

90
00:06:03,238 --> 00:06:04,239
Yum.

91
00:06:04,739 --> 00:06:06,449
The potatoes are fluffy.

92
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:07,534
Heck yes!

93
00:06:08,118 --> 00:06:09,828
The meat isn't tough either.

94
00:06:09,911 --> 00:06:10,912
-Yup.
-Yeah.

95
00:06:12,747 --> 00:06:14,249
Everything's cooked to perfection.

96
00:06:18,044 --> 00:06:20,171
[Shizu] You know how you randomly
wake up at night sometimes?

97
00:06:20,255 --> 00:06:21,256
-[Chaka] Sure.
-[Umi] Yeah.

98
00:06:21,339 --> 00:06:25,010
If I wake up tonight,
then it'll be to your sleeping faces.

99
00:06:25,093 --> 00:06:27,762
-Except when we're sleeping, we look…
-Like little angels.

100
00:06:28,263 --> 00:06:30,140
-Was that memorable?
-It really is nice.

101
00:06:30,223 --> 00:06:31,683
We work together,

102
00:06:31,766 --> 00:06:34,144
but it's not like we're ever
together for bedtime.

103
00:06:34,227 --> 00:06:35,353
[Umi] Right.

104
00:06:35,437 --> 00:06:37,647
That only happens when
we're on a trip together like this.

105
00:06:38,273 --> 00:06:40,317
-We're usually awake at work.
-You get my drift.

106
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,401
[laughs] Well, that too.

107
00:06:41,484 --> 00:06:43,486
-It'd be crazy if we dozed off at work.
-Well, yeah.

108
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:44,571
Still, it's nice.

109
00:06:44,654 --> 00:06:47,157
[Chaka] The vibe changes depending on
who's around.

110
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,491
When it's just me and Shizuya,

111
00:06:48,575 --> 00:06:50,577
or just you two,
or the three of us together,

112
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:53,079
-the vibe is a bit different.
-[Umi] It's a little different, yeah.

113
00:06:53,163 --> 00:06:57,584
So far, the good far outweighs the bad.

114
00:06:57,667 --> 00:06:58,668
[Shizu] Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

115
00:06:58,752 --> 00:07:01,630
This is day four,
but I still feel that way.

116
00:07:01,713 --> 00:07:02,714
[Shizu] Yeah.

117
00:07:02,797 --> 00:07:04,799
We're well aware of
each other's shortcomings.

118
00:07:05,467 --> 00:07:06,468
[Umi] It's true.

119
00:07:07,302 --> 00:07:08,970
[fire crackles]

120
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:12,641
[Shizu] Phew, hot!

121
00:07:14,976 --> 00:07:16,478
-Umi, is yours done?
-Not yet.

122
00:07:16,561 --> 00:07:19,105
-It's ready to come off the fire isn't it?
-No, you want it almost burned.

123
00:07:19,189 --> 00:07:21,149
That's the whole point
of toasting marshmallow.

124
00:07:21,232 --> 00:07:24,361
-Or else you're making warmed marshmallow.
-[laughs]

125
00:07:24,444 --> 00:07:26,446
-A little burnt is good?
-It's bubbly! There you have it.

126
00:07:26,529 --> 00:07:27,530
Nice and toasty.

127
00:07:29,199 --> 00:07:30,700
[Umi] Say, I had a thought.

128
00:07:30,784 --> 00:07:31,785
-[Chaka] Yeah?
-[Shizu] Okay.

129
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:37,624
I wonder why Noel
paired up the three of us.

130
00:07:38,667 --> 00:07:39,668
Good question.

131
00:07:40,835 --> 00:07:41,836
I wonder why.

132
00:07:43,546 --> 00:07:44,547
Maybe…

133
00:07:46,925 --> 00:07:49,302
He wanted to see the three of us united.

134
00:07:49,386 --> 00:07:51,554
Yeah. Good guess.

135
00:07:51,638 --> 00:07:52,639
We can only guess.

136
00:07:54,724 --> 00:07:56,393
We've been surrounded by
a lot of nature on this trip.

137
00:07:56,476 --> 00:07:58,144
-Right.
-Camping too.

138
00:07:58,228 --> 00:08:00,397
-It almost feels like we're off the grid.
-True.

139
00:08:01,064 --> 00:08:02,065
Then…

140
00:08:03,733 --> 00:08:08,321
We've had to trek through the wilderness
with our own two feet.

141
00:08:08,405 --> 00:08:09,739
-So maybe…
-Right.

142
00:08:11,282 --> 00:08:15,120
Maybe he wanted us to learn
to rely on each other.

143
00:08:15,203 --> 00:08:18,289
-Ah.
-And that's why he chose nature for us.

144
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:21,835
We might develop
new perspectives to things.

145
00:08:21,918 --> 00:08:22,919
[Shizu] Right.

146
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:24,671
-And make new discoveries.
-Right, I agree.

147
00:08:24,754 --> 00:08:28,925
-We don't know his exact reasoning yet.
-[Shizu] Yeah.

148
00:08:31,678 --> 00:08:32,929
Which probably means…

149
00:08:35,098 --> 00:08:37,934
that we haven't quite found it.

150
00:08:38,017 --> 00:08:39,018
Right.

151
00:08:40,228 --> 00:08:44,733
That's why I hope we'll find the answer

152
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:47,360
by the time our trip comes to an end.

153
00:08:47,444 --> 00:08:48,945
[Shizu] Right.
We'll come to learn the truth then.

154
00:08:49,029 --> 00:08:50,613
-[Chaka] Hopefully we can figure it out.
-[Umi] Right.

155
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,033
[Noel] Yes, that's it.

156
00:08:53,116 --> 00:08:56,411
I carefully picked out these locations
because I wanted everyone

157
00:08:56,494 --> 00:08:59,456
to set their sights
on the path ahead for Travis Japan.

158
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:04,669
There's no better place on earth
for meditation than here, after all.

159
00:09:05,545 --> 00:09:07,380
You're due to discover
what I mean tomorrow.

160
00:09:07,464 --> 00:09:09,132
I can't wait to see how you'll react.

161
00:09:13,928 --> 00:09:15,346
-[in English] Good morning.
-Good morning.

162
00:09:15,430 --> 00:09:16,598
Hi. Good afternoon.

163
00:09:16,681 --> 00:09:18,433
-[in Japanese] Today, we're already here…
-Yup.

164
00:09:19,476 --> 00:09:21,978
…at Monument Valley Tribal Park.

165
00:09:22,062 --> 00:09:23,104
-Whoa…
-Here we are.

166
00:09:23,188 --> 00:09:24,939
This is Monument Valley.

167
00:09:25,023 --> 00:09:26,232
-How amazing.
-Seriously.

168
00:09:26,316 --> 00:09:28,151
-We arrived at Monument Valley last night.
-Yeah.

169
00:09:28,234 --> 00:09:30,361
Let's see what Noel's itinerary
has in store for us today.

170
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:34,240
[Noel] Monument Valley sits
within the Navajo Nation.

171
00:09:34,324 --> 00:09:35,909
Which is the name of the largest

172
00:09:35,992 --> 00:09:38,828
Native American reservation and tribe
in the United States.

173
00:09:39,996 --> 00:09:44,417
The Navajo Tribe has resided
on this sacred land since 2,000 BC.

174
00:09:45,168 --> 00:09:47,921
With a Navajo guide by your side,

175
00:09:48,004 --> 00:09:51,299
you are allowed access
to a restricted area.

176
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,428
I want the three members to encounter
the raw Navajo Tribe culture.

177
00:09:55,512 --> 00:09:58,765
Or should I say, the raw America.

178
00:09:59,265 --> 00:10:02,060
[in English] I know you guys probably…
the first time here in Monument Valley?

179
00:10:02,143 --> 00:10:03,603
-[in English] Yes, first time.
-Yes.

180
00:10:03,686 --> 00:10:06,731
This is our motherland. Homeland. Welcome.

181
00:10:07,232 --> 00:10:08,233
Thank you so much.

182
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:11,319
Um, since you guys are here
the first time,

183
00:10:11,402 --> 00:10:15,949
um, the proper way of
who we are as people…

184
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:18,535
We call ourself Diné. Diné.

185
00:10:18,618 --> 00:10:19,994
-Diné.
-Yes.

186
00:10:20,870 --> 00:10:24,415
The Navajo name is more of a…
a secondary name and all that.

187
00:10:24,499 --> 00:10:26,876
It was given to us.
Our original name is Diné.

188
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:28,128
-Diné.
-Yes.

189
00:10:28,211 --> 00:10:32,090
Well, are you guys ready to have
some adventure today in Monument Valley?

190
00:10:32,173 --> 00:10:33,174
-Sure.
-Yeah.

191
00:10:33,258 --> 00:10:34,467
We can step in right here.

192
00:10:34,551 --> 00:10:37,303
-Yay! Let's go!
-Yeah.

193
00:10:38,221 --> 00:10:39,389
That's… Can we…

194
00:10:39,472 --> 00:10:40,473
Yeah.

195
00:10:45,728 --> 00:10:47,313
[Umi, Shizu, Chaka laugh]

196
00:10:54,112 --> 00:10:56,322
[in Japanese] Whoa, this is unreal.

197
00:10:57,157 --> 00:10:59,117
[Shizu, in English]
That's adventure. [laughs]

198
00:11:03,246 --> 00:11:05,123
[Noel, in Japanese]
Our guide is Mr. Harold Simpson,

199
00:11:05,206 --> 00:11:06,624
a Diné descendant and storyteller.

200
00:11:07,542 --> 00:11:12,797
Mr. Simpson, who grew up on
this sacred land called Monument Valley,

201
00:11:12,881 --> 00:11:15,967
recounts the memories of the Diné
through the language and the songs

202
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:18,052
passed down by his forefathers.

203
00:11:19,554 --> 00:11:20,555
[Shizu whoops]

204
00:11:20,638 --> 00:11:23,057
-[Umi] Destination. Did we reach it?
-[Shizu] What a nice day.

205
00:11:26,311 --> 00:11:28,730
[Chaka, in English] Wow. Huge.

206
00:11:28,813 --> 00:11:29,814
[Shizu] Whoa.

207
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,904
[Harold, in English] This is a place
called Mitten Buttes. Okay?

208
00:11:35,987 --> 00:11:37,155
Before I get into it,

209
00:11:37,238 --> 00:11:40,575
I'm pretty sure everything that you
see here is fabulous and all that.

210
00:11:40,658 --> 00:11:42,160
Wonderful, beautiful and all that.

211
00:11:42,243 --> 00:11:46,456
In… My introduction
to the Navajo way of life is…

212
00:11:46,539 --> 00:11:48,333
[speaks Navajo]

213
00:11:52,795 --> 00:11:54,797
[in English] My introduction
would be my mother's clan

214
00:11:54,881 --> 00:11:56,090
is the Before Dawn People.

215
00:11:56,633 --> 00:11:59,219
My father's clan
is the Bitter Water clan people.

216
00:11:59,302 --> 00:12:02,931
My maternal grandfather's clan
is the Red House Towering People clan.

217
00:12:03,556 --> 00:12:07,518
My paternal grandfather's clan
is the Yucca Fruit People clan.

218
00:12:07,602 --> 00:12:10,188
And those four clan
plays a big part in my life.

219
00:12:10,271 --> 00:12:12,690
And that's how we
introduce ourself with people.

220
00:12:17,278 --> 00:12:18,780
[tour guide speaks indistinctly]

221
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:22,909
Hey!

222
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:27,789
-That's my brother.
-Yeah, really?

223
00:12:27,872 --> 00:12:29,207
-Hi, nice to meet you.
-Richard.

224
00:12:29,290 --> 00:12:30,291
Yeah, Richard.

225
00:12:32,252 --> 00:12:34,712
-This rock that sits right over here?
-[Chaka] Mm-hmm.

226
00:12:34,796 --> 00:12:36,881
It is called… [speaks Navajo]

227
00:12:36,965 --> 00:12:39,092
-[Chaka speaks Navajo]
-[speaks Navajo]

228
00:12:39,175 --> 00:12:41,803
-[Chaka, in English] Tree?
-[Harold speaks Navajo]

229
00:12:41,886 --> 00:12:43,388
[Harold, in English] …means, um, "red."

230
00:12:43,471 --> 00:12:45,890
-[in English] Red?
-Yeah, or "downward."

231
00:12:45,974 --> 00:12:47,183
[speaks Navajo]

232
00:12:47,267 --> 00:12:49,102
[Harold, in English]
Means going up, down into the canyon.

233
00:12:49,185 --> 00:12:51,062
-[Umi, in English] Uh-huh.
-[Harold] So this mesa…

234
00:12:51,896 --> 00:12:54,691
Where that mesa is at,
it goes back into the ground.

235
00:12:54,774 --> 00:12:55,775
[Chaka] Uh-huh.

236
00:12:55,858 --> 00:12:58,528
[Harold] Weaves back.
So it comes up from here, like…

237
00:12:58,611 --> 00:13:00,571
-Like this. And it goes back down.
-[Umi] Uh-huh.

238
00:13:00,655 --> 00:13:05,493
So the definition to it is…
the description is… [speaks Navajo]

239
00:13:05,576 --> 00:13:06,661
[Chaka speaks Navajo]

240
00:13:06,744 --> 00:13:08,746
[Harold, in English] Yeah, it means
"goes back into the ground."

241
00:13:08,830 --> 00:13:10,081
[Chaka, in English] Into the ground.

242
00:13:10,915 --> 00:13:13,293
[Harold] Um, we're here again.

243
00:13:13,376 --> 00:13:15,044
This is called the "Red Land"

244
00:13:15,128 --> 00:13:17,130
or in Navajo we call it… [speaks Navajo]

245
00:13:17,213 --> 00:13:18,923
-[in English] The "Red Land". Yes.
-[speak Navajo]

246
00:13:19,007 --> 00:13:22,510
-No vegetation too much. Everything red.
-[Chaka, Umi, Shizu, in English] Mm-hmm.

247
00:13:22,593 --> 00:13:24,387
The one that we have behind.

248
00:13:24,470 --> 00:13:25,888
What does it look like to you guys?

249
00:13:25,972 --> 00:13:29,058
[Chaka, in English]
That's… that's a family.

250
00:13:29,142 --> 00:13:30,852
-[Harold] A family?
-[Chaka laughs]

251
00:13:30,935 --> 00:13:35,106
-Dad and Mom and children. It's kids.
-Oh.

252
00:13:35,189 --> 00:13:37,066
-[in Japanese] Looks like a "W" to me.
-[Chaka] It's a W.

253
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:40,320
[Harold, in English] Yeah, they're called
the… In Navajo we call it… [speaks Navajo]

254
00:13:40,403 --> 00:13:43,573
[all speak Navajo]

255
00:13:43,656 --> 00:13:46,743
[Harold, in English]
"Many rocks standing upward." Okay?

256
00:13:47,243 --> 00:13:51,039
But in English translation, we call…
It's called the "Three Sisters".

257
00:13:51,122 --> 00:13:54,917
-[in English] Three Sisters. Uh-huh.
-Like the three Catholic nuns.

258
00:13:55,001 --> 00:13:56,002
-[Harold] Mm-hmm.
-Sisters.

259
00:13:56,085 --> 00:13:57,795
[Harold] But it also looks like a…

260
00:13:57,879 --> 00:13:59,756
a W and a V.

261
00:13:59,839 --> 00:14:03,301
"Welcome visitor to wonderful valley.
Enjoy the wonderful views."

262
00:14:03,384 --> 00:14:05,094
-Wonderful.
-Wonderful. W.

263
00:14:05,178 --> 00:14:07,138
[laughs] Got ya.

264
00:14:07,221 --> 00:14:09,182
-Uh-huh.
-[Shizu] Thank you. [laughs]

265
00:14:09,265 --> 00:14:12,351
Okay, and then we have this
right over here.

266
00:14:12,435 --> 00:14:13,436
[Umi] Mm-hmm.

267
00:14:13,519 --> 00:14:15,521
[Harold] This is called
the John Ford's Point.

268
00:14:15,605 --> 00:14:18,483
-[Umi] Point.
-In Navajo we call it… [speaks Navajo]

269
00:14:18,566 --> 00:14:19,901
[all speak Navajo]

270
00:14:19,984 --> 00:14:22,779
-[in English] The "Pointed Rock." Okay?
-[Umi, in English] Mm-hmm.

271
00:14:23,279 --> 00:14:26,699
But then 1920,
Harry Goulding came out here

272
00:14:26,783 --> 00:14:31,204
and introduced a movie director
by the name of, uh, John Ford,

273
00:14:31,287 --> 00:14:34,665
and started the movies
out here in the '30s and so on.

274
00:14:34,749 --> 00:14:38,586
The Searchers, She Wore a Yellow Ribbon
and Mackenna's Gold.

275
00:14:38,669 --> 00:14:42,632
The list goes on. And so many movies
has been made here and all that.

276
00:14:42,715 --> 00:14:46,427
So, he filmed it from there
and he loved that area,

277
00:14:46,511 --> 00:14:48,721
and that's why it's called
the John Ford's Point.

278
00:14:48,805 --> 00:14:49,806
Mm-hmm.

279
00:14:52,934 --> 00:14:54,435
[in Japanese] During the trip,

280
00:14:54,519 --> 00:14:57,855
we've seen a lot of jewelry
made with precious gemstones

281
00:14:57,939 --> 00:15:00,233
mined from this region.

282
00:15:00,316 --> 00:15:05,613
Could you tell us what significance
these precious stones have

283
00:15:06,114 --> 00:15:07,740
in your culture?

284
00:15:07,824 --> 00:15:08,825
[Harold, in English] Yes.

285
00:15:08,908 --> 00:15:11,744
Um, to the Navajo people and all that,
Diné people,

286
00:15:12,286 --> 00:15:15,164
rock is very close to us and so on.

287
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:18,501
So when we purchase something,
like jewelries and all that,

288
00:15:18,584 --> 00:15:20,795
turquoise, silvers, or anything like that,

289
00:15:20,878 --> 00:15:24,340
we don't put it in a safe place
or storage.

290
00:15:24,841 --> 00:15:29,220
The reason by it is because we are
in need of it. Then it helps us heal.

291
00:15:29,887 --> 00:15:33,432
And turquoise is very special to us
and all that.

292
00:15:33,516 --> 00:15:39,981
And then it makes us more outgoing,
more energetic and so on.

293
00:15:40,064 --> 00:15:44,527
As well with sterling silver as well.
We're more grounded that way and so on.

294
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:47,697
And that's one of the reasons
and the philosophy of the Navajo people…

295
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:51,242
If we have jewelries,
they also need to be breathing.

296
00:15:51,325 --> 00:15:54,871
They also need to socialize
with other jewelries as well.

297
00:15:54,954 --> 00:15:56,372
And so we wear it all the time.

298
00:15:56,455 --> 00:15:59,500
-[in English] Uh-huh. It's interesting.
-[Umi] Yeah, interesting.

299
00:16:00,334 --> 00:16:06,299
Our body choose for a rock or any element,
minerals that we need.

300
00:16:06,382 --> 00:16:09,010
Just like the one that
you're wearing right now.

301
00:16:09,093 --> 00:16:12,930
Your body needs that so you…
attracts to that.

302
00:16:13,014 --> 00:16:14,473
And then you're wearing it right now.

303
00:16:14,557 --> 00:16:16,726
Because everybody
in the Navajo way of life,

304
00:16:17,226 --> 00:16:19,061
-we need to be balanced.
-[Shizu] Mmm.

305
00:16:19,145 --> 00:16:20,396
"Beauty Way" we call it.

306
00:16:20,479 --> 00:16:23,232
Beauty before me, beauty behind me,
beauty above me,

307
00:16:23,316 --> 00:16:25,568
beauty beside me, beauty around me.

308
00:16:25,651 --> 00:16:30,656
And so the way we heal
is by elements or stones

309
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:34,994
or anything that can heal us internally
and/or physically as well.

310
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:36,662
-[in Japanese] I had no idea.
-How interesting.

311
00:16:36,746 --> 00:16:38,331
[in English] I see that. [laughs]

312
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:40,124
[in Japanese] Whoa.

313
00:16:40,208 --> 00:16:44,503
[in English] That's little surprise to us.
That… Those things.

314
00:16:47,423 --> 00:16:49,342
[Harold] Yeah, as you can see, that…

315
00:16:49,425 --> 00:16:52,094
the cloud is above us,
the rain coming down and all that.

316
00:16:52,178 --> 00:16:55,598
We don't get as much rain
as we're supposed to and all that,

317
00:16:55,681 --> 00:17:00,770
so our precipitation about 30 years ago
was about, uh, 30 centimeters.

318
00:17:01,395 --> 00:17:04,690
Now we are lucky to have
about 15 centimeters of rain and all that.

319
00:17:04,774 --> 00:17:05,775
-Wow.
-Throughout the year.

320
00:17:05,858 --> 00:17:08,569
-Yeah.
-Our precipitation is very, very scarce.

321
00:17:08,653 --> 00:17:10,404
-Uh-huh.
-So when we see, um…

322
00:17:10,488 --> 00:17:14,158
um, rain coming down, okay, we enjoy it.
And we uplift and all that.

323
00:17:14,242 --> 00:17:15,326
[Chaka, in English] Oh, I see.

324
00:17:15,409 --> 00:17:16,410
Yeah.

325
00:17:16,494 --> 00:17:19,622
And then right now,
once we get back in the vehicle,

326
00:17:19,705 --> 00:17:22,833
we're gonna go to an area
that we call a restricted area.

327
00:17:22,917 --> 00:17:24,377
-[Shizu] Restricted area.
-Uh, yes.

328
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:27,463
No public vehicle goes out there
and all that, so…

329
00:17:27,546 --> 00:17:29,382
-Go. Let's go.
-A special place.

330
00:17:29,465 --> 00:17:31,133
-Yeah. Whoa!
-Yes.

331
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:32,635
-Let's go.
-Let's follow you.

332
00:17:32,718 --> 00:17:34,220
Let's go, boys.

333
00:17:34,303 --> 00:17:36,973
-Can you smell it now?
-[Chaka] Yeah, yeah, good. Rain smell.

334
00:17:37,557 --> 00:17:39,934
-[Umi, in Japanese] Amazing. Mmm.
-[Chaka, in English] That's good smell.

335
00:17:40,434 --> 00:17:42,728
-[Shizu] It's the smell of the rain.
-[Chaka] I can feel ground.

336
00:17:42,812 --> 00:17:43,813
[Umi] Yeah.

337
00:17:45,523 --> 00:17:47,108
[rain pattering]

338
00:17:52,196 --> 00:17:53,364
[in Japanese] How incredible.

339
00:17:53,447 --> 00:17:56,534
[in English] We bring rain to here.

340
00:17:57,326 --> 00:17:58,327
[Harold] Yes.

341
00:17:58,953 --> 00:18:00,788
[rain pattering continues]

342
00:18:26,147 --> 00:18:28,524
-[Harold] Look at the view right here.
-[Umi, Chaka, in English] Mm-hmm.

343
00:18:28,608 --> 00:18:32,194
Everywhere you turn in Monument Valley,
it's always magical.

344
00:18:32,278 --> 00:18:35,740
-Mm-hmm.
-And every place has a hidden area

345
00:18:35,823 --> 00:18:38,868
that is very special
to everybody and all that, so…

346
00:18:38,951 --> 00:18:41,746
This is called the "North Window".

347
00:18:41,829 --> 00:18:43,706
-North Window.
-Yes.

348
00:18:43,789 --> 00:18:44,999
[Chaka] Oh.

349
00:18:45,082 --> 00:18:46,375
This is my homeland.

350
00:18:46,459 --> 00:18:49,712
This is where my ancestral people
came from, Diné people.

351
00:18:50,296 --> 00:18:53,966
My grandparents and their grandparents
and pass on.

352
00:18:54,050 --> 00:18:55,760
My grand… And my parents as well.

353
00:18:55,843 --> 00:18:58,804
So they resided out here
and lived here most of their life,

354
00:18:58,888 --> 00:19:00,306
and lived in this area.

355
00:19:00,389 --> 00:19:03,643
So as a child I explored this area

356
00:19:03,726 --> 00:19:06,729
and I did a lot of wonderful things
out here.

357
00:19:06,812 --> 00:19:09,315
And when I was younger
and all that, so it's…

358
00:19:09,398 --> 00:19:10,900
It means a lot to me.

359
00:19:10,983 --> 00:19:15,363
When I go away, I always want to come back
and look at it again and so on.

360
00:19:34,131 --> 00:19:36,634
[distant jingling]

361
00:19:40,096 --> 00:19:42,556
-[in Japanese] Do you hear the jingling?
-What's that?

362
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:44,058
[jingling continues]

363
00:19:48,729 --> 00:19:50,523
[Umi, Shime, Chaka, in English] Whoa!

364
00:19:52,233 --> 00:19:54,402
-[Umi, in Japanese] Amazing.
-[Chaka, in English] So cool!

365
00:19:56,570 --> 00:19:57,571
[Umi, in Japanese] Cool!

366
00:19:58,989 --> 00:20:00,074
[drum thumps]

367
00:20:00,157 --> 00:20:01,242
[clears throat] Here it goes.

368
00:20:01,325 --> 00:20:03,202
-[drum thumping]
-[ankle bells jingling]

369
00:20:03,285 --> 00:20:04,286
[Chaka, in Japanese] Wow.

370
00:20:05,454 --> 00:20:06,789
[Harold sings in Navajo]

371
00:20:10,334 --> 00:20:12,503
[singing continues]

372
00:20:12,586 --> 00:20:16,424
[Harold, in English]
Prior to 1868, we protected the land,

373
00:20:16,507 --> 00:20:18,300
we protect the families and so on.

374
00:20:18,926 --> 00:20:21,303
But after June 1st 1868,

375
00:20:21,387 --> 00:20:24,932
we signed a treaty with the government
that we were never going to fight again.

376
00:20:25,516 --> 00:20:28,185
So after that, things changed for us.

377
00:20:28,269 --> 00:20:35,025
But prior to 1960-- 1868, excuse me, 1868,
protecting the land and family…

378
00:20:35,109 --> 00:20:40,448
that's what we use and obtain and all that
so the, the people become warriors.

379
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:44,285
They have to be brave enough
to go into an enemy camp

380
00:20:44,785 --> 00:20:47,580
and get something from the enemy camp
and bring it back.

381
00:20:48,164 --> 00:20:49,915
And that's how they earned their feathers.

382
00:20:50,791 --> 00:20:53,169
Majority of the people,
they like to do prayers.

383
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:56,964
Early morning prayers.
Afternoon, evening prayers and so on.

384
00:20:57,047 --> 00:21:01,677
They… They want some kind of
an affirmation in their life and so on.

385
00:21:02,803 --> 00:21:05,806
[drumming, singing continue]

386
00:21:31,874 --> 00:21:35,461
So the moral of the stories back then,

387
00:21:35,544 --> 00:21:38,172
a lot of Native American raided
each other and…

388
00:21:38,255 --> 00:21:41,759
But during the Spanish era and all that,

389
00:21:41,842 --> 00:21:45,846
they came out here
and tried to invade the Native Americans.

390
00:21:45,930 --> 00:21:48,974
And so by force we had
to take them back out there and all that.

391
00:21:49,058 --> 00:21:51,602
But they also introduced
their horses to us

392
00:21:51,685 --> 00:21:53,854
and that's how we're trying to obtain it.

393
00:21:53,938 --> 00:21:57,650
So through lightning and thunder,
we obtain the horses and all that.

394
00:21:57,733 --> 00:21:59,652
Thunder and all that. So,

395
00:21:59,735 --> 00:22:03,364
as rain come down with all that water
coming down, with all the lightning,

396
00:22:03,447 --> 00:22:05,282
with all the thunders and all that,

397
00:22:05,366 --> 00:22:08,327
that was our advantage for us
to take the horses

398
00:22:08,410 --> 00:22:10,162
without being detected at nighttime.

399
00:22:10,746 --> 00:22:13,082
So the lightning come give us a flash.

400
00:22:13,165 --> 00:22:15,626
The light that we need.
The noise that the horse make,

401
00:22:15,709 --> 00:22:17,878
the thunders and everything blends it out.

402
00:22:17,962 --> 00:22:22,132
And then the rain when we ride them out,
the rain just washes everything out.

403
00:22:22,216 --> 00:22:24,552
And that's how
we obtained the horses and all that.

404
00:22:24,635 --> 00:22:28,055
So this song is pertaining to that
and all that so…

405
00:22:28,138 --> 00:22:29,265
[drumming, singing ends]

406
00:22:29,974 --> 00:22:33,936
[in English] Why did you decide the…
do the dancing?

407
00:22:34,562 --> 00:22:39,650
Well, I wanna get connected
to my Native… Native fate.

408
00:22:40,150 --> 00:22:45,573
So I just think that has you become
more connected with your nature

409
00:22:45,656 --> 00:22:48,325
or your Native nature is…

410
00:22:49,118 --> 00:22:52,079
just grass dance or powwow dancing.

411
00:22:52,162 --> 00:22:53,414
It connects with people.

412
00:22:53,497 --> 00:22:57,376
Helps you connect with earth
as you meet all kinds of people

413
00:22:57,459 --> 00:23:00,129
from other tribes and many other things.

414
00:23:00,212 --> 00:23:04,383
So I wanted to be connected. Just have
my regular trace, follow my culture.

415
00:23:04,466 --> 00:23:05,718
[Umi, in English] Hmm.

416
00:23:05,801 --> 00:23:08,012
-[Chaka, in English] I respect you.
-[Umi] Yeah.

417
00:23:09,221 --> 00:23:10,431
[Chaka] That's great.

418
00:23:10,514 --> 00:23:11,932
-[Shizu] Very nice.
-[Chaka] That was amazing.

419
00:23:12,016 --> 00:23:13,017
-Yeah.
-Yes.

420
00:23:14,018 --> 00:23:15,185
Thank you so much.

421
00:23:15,269 --> 00:23:16,604
-[Chaka] You wanna shake hand?
-Yeah.

422
00:23:16,687 --> 00:23:17,855
-[Chaka] Thank you.
-[Shizu] Thank you.

423
00:23:18,355 --> 00:23:19,732
-[Umi] Thank you so much.
-You're welcome.

424
00:23:19,815 --> 00:23:21,567
-[Shizu] You're a great dancer.
-[Chaka] Yeah.

425
00:23:21,650 --> 00:23:23,235
-[Shizu] Thank you.
-[Harold] You're welcome.

426
00:23:24,820 --> 00:23:26,822
[in Japanese] These traditional…

427
00:23:26,906 --> 00:23:27,907
[clears throat]

428
00:23:27,990 --> 00:23:31,911
…dance and song are how the Navajo tribe
have passed down their culture

429
00:23:31,994 --> 00:23:34,830
from one generation to the next.
Over time, it painted their history.

430
00:23:34,914 --> 00:23:35,915
Yeah.

431
00:23:35,998 --> 00:23:38,000
I got to experience
their culture firsthand.

432
00:23:40,544 --> 00:23:43,339
-They express this breathtaking nature…
-Yeah.

433
00:23:43,422 --> 00:23:45,424
…through music and dance. The music too.

434
00:23:45,507 --> 00:23:51,055
It's vastly different from the pop music
we create, or what's trending.

435
00:23:51,138 --> 00:23:53,057
-No.
-Yeah, there's nothing quite like it.

436
00:23:53,140 --> 00:23:58,103
It's their rendition of the music
bestowed upon them by Mother Earth.

437
00:23:58,687 --> 00:24:02,691
-Such a refreshing spin on music.
-Mm-hmm.

438
00:24:02,775 --> 00:24:03,776
I agree.

439
00:24:03,859 --> 00:24:05,945
-It was a breath of fresh air.
-Right.

440
00:24:06,028 --> 00:24:09,365
What an incredible,
once-in-a-lifetime experience.

441
00:24:09,448 --> 00:24:10,532
I agree.

442
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:12,242
Right?

443
00:24:12,993 --> 00:24:17,790
That dance blurs the boundary
between tradition and entertainment.

444
00:24:17,873 --> 00:24:20,751
But dance and music are universal,
you know?

445
00:24:20,834 --> 00:24:22,169
I agree. Yes.

446
00:24:22,252 --> 00:24:24,588
It left me with a sense of wonder.

447
00:24:24,672 --> 00:24:26,465
We live in a vast world.

448
00:24:27,341 --> 00:24:28,676
It's full of wonders
we've yet to discover.

449
00:24:28,759 --> 00:24:29,760
I agree.

450
00:24:31,887 --> 00:24:33,389
[♪ serene music plays]

451
00:24:53,909 --> 00:24:55,411
[♪ music continues]

452
00:25:34,700 --> 00:25:36,201
[♪ music continues]

453
00:26:09,735 --> 00:26:10,736
[♪ music stops]

454
00:26:11,862 --> 00:26:14,865
[♪ Harold sings in Navajo]

455
00:26:33,884 --> 00:26:35,886
[♪ singing continues]

456
00:26:57,574 --> 00:26:58,742
[♪ singing stops]

457
00:26:58,826 --> 00:26:59,868
[Harold exclaims]

458
00:27:02,538 --> 00:27:04,540
[exclaims]

459
00:27:04,623 --> 00:27:06,625
[exclaims]

460
00:27:08,210 --> 00:27:10,212
[exclaims]

461
00:27:11,672 --> 00:27:13,674
[distant exclaiming]

462
00:27:47,666 --> 00:27:48,667
Hello.

463
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:51,795
[in English] Hello. Nice to meet you.
I'm Chaka. Nice to meet you.

464
00:27:51,879 --> 00:27:52,880
I'm Shizu.

465
00:27:52,963 --> 00:27:54,131
Nice to meet you. I'm Umi.

466
00:27:54,214 --> 00:27:55,215
I'm Rahelio.

467
00:27:55,299 --> 00:27:57,175
Rahelio. Nice to meet you.

468
00:27:57,259 --> 00:27:59,553
-I'm gonna take you someplace special, so…
-Oh, really?

469
00:27:59,636 --> 00:28:02,431
Vibration through your body.
Take you into a meditation experience.

470
00:28:02,514 --> 00:28:03,724
-Meditation.
-Meditation, yeah.

471
00:28:04,558 --> 00:28:06,602
[Rahelio]
This is the Thunder Mountain right here.

472
00:28:06,685 --> 00:28:08,520
-[Umi] Mm-hmm.
-[Chaka] Whoa.

473
00:28:08,604 --> 00:28:13,275
[Rahelio] The Sky Father speaks
with thunder, so this is like this…

474
00:28:13,358 --> 00:28:15,652
Very powerful place.
Lightning hits here all the time.

475
00:28:15,736 --> 00:28:17,404
It's a very powerful mountain.

476
00:28:17,487 --> 00:28:20,532
And we're gonna go down, do
a little blessing ceremony and a little…

477
00:28:21,116 --> 00:28:23,201
and a meditation for you.
And kind of guide you.

478
00:28:24,995 --> 00:28:26,246
[in Japanese] Look, a mountain path.

479
00:28:29,499 --> 00:28:30,500
[Umi] Amazing.

480
00:28:35,505 --> 00:28:37,341
[Rahelio vocalizes]

481
00:28:37,424 --> 00:28:39,426
It's like walking on the Mother Earth.

482
00:28:39,927 --> 00:28:41,845
Mother Earth is a living consciousness.

483
00:28:42,346 --> 00:28:45,432
Human beings have forgotten
that the Mother Earth is alive.

484
00:28:45,933 --> 00:28:47,559
As we become more conscious with her,

485
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:50,646
uh, we'll have much more prosperity,
more success.

486
00:28:51,188 --> 00:28:54,900
Right now the world… people are exploiting
the earth and treating it as a commodity.

487
00:28:54,983 --> 00:28:57,819
But the earth is living,
and as we walk the wheel…

488
00:28:57,903 --> 00:29:00,656
[all vocalizing]

489
00:29:06,536 --> 00:29:08,622
[Rahelio] I built this wheel 21 years ago.

490
00:29:09,289 --> 00:29:11,416
When we walk the wheel,
we're walking a prayer.

491
00:29:11,500 --> 00:29:12,584
[Chaka, in English] Mm-hmm.

492
00:29:12,668 --> 00:29:16,213
It's like we're walking prayerfulness
to bless our families,

493
00:29:16,296 --> 00:29:19,883
our communities, our nations.
To bless all sentient beings.

494
00:29:21,843 --> 00:29:23,845
[all vocalizing]

495
00:29:35,691 --> 00:29:36,942
Each step is a prayer.

496
00:29:41,238 --> 00:29:43,156
And where you stop
will be a teaching for you,

497
00:29:43,240 --> 00:29:46,827
a blessing for you where you stop.
On the wheel.

498
00:29:48,704 --> 00:29:50,664
This always seems
to be my place to stop right here,

499
00:29:50,747 --> 00:29:52,290
so stop wherever you feel right.

500
00:30:05,554 --> 00:30:07,264
The wheel is like the galaxy.

501
00:30:08,056 --> 00:30:09,641
Great wheel of stars.

502
00:30:12,978 --> 00:30:15,522
He stands in the east,
a place of new beginning.

503
00:30:16,523 --> 00:30:18,275
"Who am I? Where am I going? What…"

504
00:30:18,775 --> 00:30:22,029
A coming forth
with a new part of your life. Growth.

505
00:30:22,112 --> 00:30:23,697
When is your birthday?

506
00:30:23,780 --> 00:30:25,407
-April.
-[Rahelio] A Taurus?

507
00:30:25,907 --> 00:30:27,951
-Fifteen.
-[Rahelio] Aries?

508
00:30:28,035 --> 00:30:29,202
-Yeah.
-[Rahelio] Aries.

509
00:30:29,286 --> 00:30:32,039
Aries, in Native American zodiac,
is Red Hawk.

510
00:30:32,122 --> 00:30:33,707
Red Hawk people. Red Hawk.

511
00:30:33,790 --> 00:30:37,627
Quick to fly and, "See if I like this.
I'm in. I don't like it. I'm outta here."

512
00:30:37,711 --> 00:30:40,797
It's about finding personal freedom
and self-discovery

513
00:30:40,881 --> 00:30:41,882
by just going forth

514
00:30:41,965 --> 00:30:44,384
and trusting your instincts
for what feels right for you.

515
00:30:44,468 --> 00:30:46,970
So a new beginning.
A new cycle of self-discovery.

516
00:30:47,846 --> 00:30:49,097
When's your birthday?

517
00:30:49,181 --> 00:30:50,474
August 10th.

518
00:30:51,266 --> 00:30:53,060
[Rahelio] This is the place of summer.

519
00:30:54,686 --> 00:30:58,940
The place of growth, support, security.

520
00:30:59,649 --> 00:31:02,319
A community and family connection.

521
00:31:02,819 --> 00:31:04,863
So here is all about family ties

522
00:31:04,946 --> 00:31:09,242
and feeling supported
and secure in who you are.

523
00:31:11,161 --> 00:31:12,204
When's his birthday?

524
00:31:12,287 --> 00:31:14,039
September 22nd.

525
00:31:14,122 --> 00:31:16,541
[Rahelio] A Libra.
This right here is Libra.

526
00:31:17,084 --> 00:31:22,464
This is the midsummer.
It's about creative purpose, creativity,

527
00:31:22,547 --> 00:31:24,633
"Find in my heart what I need to express."

528
00:31:24,716 --> 00:31:31,264
And as you move here, this is more about
refining… refining your life expression

529
00:31:31,348 --> 00:31:38,021
and getting things… You're, like,
working out any kind of creative block.

530
00:31:38,105 --> 00:31:40,440
You just put your energy into focus.

531
00:31:40,524 --> 00:31:46,071
So, it's taking creativity and
channeling it to, like, your perfections.

532
00:31:46,154 --> 00:31:48,156
Your… "This is what I… my routine.

533
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:50,659
This is how I wanna…
what I'm trying to create like that."

534
00:31:51,326 --> 00:31:53,328
[Rahelio, Umi vocalizing]

535
00:32:04,005 --> 00:32:06,007
[vocalizing continues]

536
00:32:15,225 --> 00:32:16,726
[vocalizing stops]

537
00:32:16,810 --> 00:32:19,146
[drum thumps]

538
00:32:20,939 --> 00:32:22,607
[Rahelio] It's the heartbeat of creation.

539
00:32:24,776 --> 00:32:26,111
[drumming continues]

540
00:32:29,698 --> 00:32:31,366
Totally relaxing.

541
00:32:41,877 --> 00:32:45,046
[Rahelio sings in Navajo]

542
00:32:53,305 --> 00:32:56,308
[drumming, singing continue]

543
00:33:06,276 --> 00:33:07,277
[drumming stops]

544
00:33:07,360 --> 00:33:10,572
-[Rahelio sings in Navajo]
-[bell chimes]

545
00:33:20,373 --> 00:33:23,460
[singing, chiming continue]

546
00:33:33,595 --> 00:33:37,599
-[rattle shakes]
-[♪ flute music plays]

547
00:33:45,607 --> 00:33:48,068
[♪ rattling, music continue]

548
00:33:56,993 --> 00:33:57,994
[♪ rattling, music stop]

549
00:33:58,578 --> 00:33:59,579
Take your time…

550
00:33:59,663 --> 00:34:01,248
-[Rahelio chants in Navajo]
-[drum thumps]

551
00:34:01,331 --> 00:34:04,084
…and come back from the meditation
whenever you want.

552
00:34:08,171 --> 00:34:11,174
[chanting, drumming continue]

553
00:34:12,342 --> 00:34:13,510
[chanting, drumming stop]

554
00:34:13,593 --> 00:34:15,053
[Rahelio speaks Navajo]

555
00:34:15,136 --> 00:34:17,472
[in English] Thank you, thank you.
Be spared. Arigatou.

556
00:34:20,100 --> 00:34:21,434
[♪ serene music plays]

557
00:34:38,076 --> 00:34:39,411
[♪ music continues]

558
00:34:48,753 --> 00:34:51,298
[in Japanese] I feel the "nothingness."

559
00:34:51,965 --> 00:34:54,801
[Rahelio, in English]
The universe came out of nothingness.

560
00:34:55,552 --> 00:34:59,097
It was nothingness, the void,
and then light came forth.

561
00:34:59,723 --> 00:35:02,892
So, out of the nothingness,
creativity comes forth.

562
00:35:03,518 --> 00:35:06,563
[in Japanese] It's, like,
my senses are resting from everything,

563
00:35:06,646 --> 00:35:09,065
and I'm in a space of my own.

564
00:35:09,774 --> 00:35:10,942
It felt serene.

565
00:35:11,026 --> 00:35:15,947
[in English] In the mountains, in nature,
there's natural energy rhythm.

566
00:35:16,823 --> 00:35:20,702
Birds singing, crickets making noise.

567
00:35:20,785 --> 00:35:24,331
Nature has rhythms, like brain waves.

568
00:35:25,415 --> 00:35:30,879
So, to be by the mountain,
by the land, in nature,

569
00:35:31,588 --> 00:35:34,299
it syncs us back to the natural self.

570
00:35:34,382 --> 00:35:36,301
That's where our creativity comes from.

571
00:35:36,926 --> 00:35:43,350
[in Japanese] The vision I had for myself
has become crystal clear again.

572
00:35:43,850 --> 00:35:44,851
I feel

573
00:35:46,770 --> 00:35:48,396
my connection to Mother Earth.

574
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:51,066
[Rahelio, in English]
You just, uh, trust in your intuition

575
00:35:51,149 --> 00:35:53,401
'cause new opportunities
are coming for you.

576
00:35:53,902 --> 00:35:57,739
So, the more you connect
with your nature within you,

577
00:35:58,406 --> 00:36:00,784
the more your guide will come forth.

578
00:36:00,867 --> 00:36:01,868
[in Japanese] Yes.

579
00:36:02,952 --> 00:36:04,454
-Thank you.
-[Rahelio, in Japanese] Thank you.

580
00:36:07,707 --> 00:36:10,543
[all singing in Navajo]

581
00:36:20,387 --> 00:36:22,389
[singing continues]

582
00:36:38,113 --> 00:36:39,114
[singing stops]

583
00:36:39,698 --> 00:36:41,449
-[Rahelio, in English] Ooh.
-[Umi, Shizu, Chaka] Ooh.

584
00:36:41,533 --> 00:36:42,867
[rattles shake]

585
00:36:42,951 --> 00:36:45,870
We're singing to the Sky Father
to bless the circle of life.

586
00:36:45,954 --> 00:36:47,789
-Up there.
-Okay.

587
00:36:47,872 --> 00:36:52,252
The world has been broken by war
and conflict and negative energy.

588
00:36:52,335 --> 00:36:54,212
So that song is
to renew the circle of life.

589
00:36:54,295 --> 00:36:55,296
[Shizu] Hmm.

590
00:36:59,217 --> 00:37:01,219
[♪ serene music plays]

591
00:37:10,353 --> 00:37:11,438
[♪ music continues]

592
00:37:12,981 --> 00:37:15,150
[Noel, in Japanese]
I wonder if everyone has had some clarity.

593
00:37:16,776 --> 00:37:19,529
I can't wait to meet up with them
and hear all their stories.

594
00:37:27,829 --> 00:37:28,997
[♪ music stops]

595
00:38:40,235 --> 00:38:42,237
Translated by Megumi Hunter
